Why does a nail infection take longer to clear than almost anything else your body deals with? That is the question most people land on this page already asking, usually after weeks of watching a toenail change colour and hoping it would just sort itself out. <strong>Toenail fungus</strong> is one of the most common nail conditions out there, and it is also one of the most misunderstood. Roughly one in ten people will deal with it at some point, and that number climbs sharply after sixty.

This guide is built differently to most of what you will find searching for <strong>toenail fungus</strong> online. Medical sites tend to list symptoms you already know without telling you what they mean for your specific situation. We are going to do the opposite: explain what is actually happening under the nail, give you an honest answer on whether it resolves alone, walk you through diagnosis confusion, and lay out a clear decision path for treatment based on how advanced things are. No padding, no scare tactics, just what you need to act with confidence.

## What Is Toenail Fungus?

A fungal infection of the nail is not a cosmetic quirk. It is a living organism that has colonised the nail and the tissue underneath it, and it behaves according to its own biology, not your schedule.

<strong>Onychomycosis</strong> is the medical term for a fungal infection of the nail, caused most commonly by dermatophytes, a group of fungi that feed on keratin. The clinical name sounds intimidating, but it simply describes what <strong>toenail fungus</strong> is at a biological level: an organism digesting the protein structure of your nail from the inside out. Doctors also call it tinea unguium, which translates loosely to "nail ringworm," even though no worm is involved. It is the same family of fungus responsible for athlete's foot, just relocated to a tougher piece of real estate.

There are a few recognised patterns of <strong>fungal nail infection</strong>, and they matter because they affect how the nail looks and how stubborn the infection tends to be. Distal lateral subungual onychomycosis, the most common form by far, starts at the tip or side of the nail and works its way toward the cuticle. White superficial onychomycosis sits on the nail's surface instead and tends to respond faster to treatment. Less commonly, Candida yeasts cause infection rather than dermatophytes, particularly in fingernails or in people with compromised immune systems. Knowing which type you have is not something you can diagnose from a search bar, but understanding that "<strong>toenail fungus</strong>" is not one single thing helps explain why two people can have wildly different experiences with the same diagnosis.

