## What Does Toenail Fungus Look Like? Symptoms by Stage

You have probably already noticed something. The question is whether what you are seeing matches what fungus actually does, or whether it is something else entirely.

<strong>Toenail fungus</strong> typically starts as a small white or yellow spot near the free edge of the nail, then spreads to cause thickening, brittleness, a crumbling edge, and a dulled or chalky surface as it progresses. Early on, the change can be so subtle it is easy to write off as a stubbed toe or a bit of nail polish residue. That is exactly the stage where a lot of people search "my toenail is yellow and thick" and assume the worst, or dismiss it entirely.

In the early stage, look for a faint discolouration at one corner or along the tip, sometimes paired with a white, streaky patch if the surface type is involved. The nail still feels mostly normal to the touch. As things progress to a mid stage, the discolouration deepens toward yellow or brown, the nail starts thickening unevenly, and you might notice debris collecting underneath where the nail has lifted slightly. Crumbling at the edges becomes more obvious here too, often described by people as the nail "flaking apart" when they trim it.

By the later stage, the picture changes substantially. The nail can separate almost entirely from the nail bed, a process called onycholysis, leaving a visible gap that traps moisture and debris. Thickness can become severe enough to press uncomfortably against shoes. Colour often deepens toward brown or develops dark streaking. This is also the stage where odour becomes noticeable, caused by the breakdown products as the fungus consumes keratin.

If you are trying to work out which stage matches what you are looking at, photographs make a real difference, since written descriptions only go so far. For a detailed visual breakdown, see our guide to <a href='/blog/toenail-fungus/toenail-fungus-stages'>toenail fungus stages</a>. One more note before moving on: a single discoloured patch that appeared suddenly after you dropped something on your toe is far more likely to be trauma, specifically a bruise under the nail, than fungus. Fungal changes build gradually. Trauma shows up fast, often within a day or two, and tends to be a solid dark patch rather than a spreading, textured one.

