## What Causes Toenail Fungus and Who Is at Risk?

Why does this particular infection seem to target feet so reliably, when fingernails barely ever get it? The environment your toes spend their day in is basically a fungal greeting card.

<strong>Dermatophytes</strong> are microscopic fungi that digest keratin, the protein that makes up nails and skin, and they thrive in warm, moist environments. Socks and shoes create exactly that environment for hours at a stretch, every single day, which is why toenails are affected so much more often than fingernails. Add a dark, enclosed space with limited airflow, and you have ideal breeding conditions that fingernails, exposed and dry most of the day, simply do not offer.

The exposure itself usually comes from a handful of predictable sources. Communal showers, locker rooms, and pool decks are classic transmission points, since fungal spores survive on damp surfaces and transfer easily to bare feet. Shared or poorly sanitised nail clippers and pedicure tools are another common route, carrying fungus from an infected nail directly to a healthy one. <strong>Is toenail fungus contagious</strong>? Yes, and one of the most overlooked pathways is athlete's foot on your own feet, where the same fungal infection spreads from the skin between your toes into the nail itself. If you have ever had persistent athlete's foot, the connection to a fungal nail later on is not a coincidence.

Certain people carry a higher baseline risk regardless of exposure. Poor circulation, common with age or peripheral vascular disease, slows the immune response that would otherwise help fight off early infection. A weakened immune system, whether from a chronic illness, certain medications, or age itself, has the same effect. Previous nail trauma creates microscopic openings fungus can exploit. And diabetes deserves its own mention here, not as a footnote but as a genuinely elevated risk factor, both for developing <strong>toenail fungus</strong> in the first place and for complications if it goes untreated, something we will come back to directly.

