## How Is Toenail Fungus Diagnosed?

Here is something that surprises a lot of people: a doctor looking at your nail cannot always tell, just by eye, whether fungus is actually the culprit.

Discolouration and thickening have several possible causes, and they overlap visually more than you would expect. Nail trauma from an old injury, certain medications, and especially nail psoriasis can all produce a yellowed, thickened, pitted nail that looks remarkably close to a fungal infection at a glance. Conditions like <strong>toenail fungus vs nail psoriasis</strong> genuinely can look nearly identical without testing, which is exactly why guessing from a mirror or a search engine has real limits.

That is where confirmation testing earns its place. A clinician will typically clip a small nail sample and either examine it under a microscope using a KOH smear, which dissolves the keratin to reveal fungal elements directly, or send it for a nail culture test that identifies the specific organism involved. The <a href='https://www.aad.org/public/diseases/a-z/nail-fungus-overview'>American Academy of Dermatology</a> notes that testing matters because treatment plans differ depending on whether the cause is a dermatophyte, a yeast, or something else altogether, including conditions that are not fungal at all.

If you have tried an over-the-counter cream for weeks with no improvement, that is often a sign you are not actually dealing with fungus, or dealing with a fungal strain the product was never going to touch. A short visit for testing saves months of guessing. It also rules out psoriasis or chronic trauma, conditions where antifungal treatment would do precisely nothing.

