## When to See a Doctor About Toenail Fungus

Is this a "deal with it eventually" problem or a "see someone this week" problem? The answer depends on a short list of specific signals, not how the nail looks in general.

See a doctor if you have diabetes or a compromised immune system, if the infection is spreading to additional nails, if you experience pain, or if an over-the-counter treatment has not worked after several months. Each of these is a genuine decision gate rather than a vague suggestion.

Diabetes changes the stakes entirely, because reduced circulation and sensation in the feet mean a minor infection can progress to skin breakdown without you noticing early warning signs like pain. Spreading is another clear trigger: a single affected nail managed at home is a very different situation from an infection visibly moving to neighbouring toes. Pain, swelling, or redness around the nail suggests a secondary bacterial infection layered on top of the fungal one, which needs prompt attention rather than more antifungal cream. And if you have genuinely used an over-the-counter product correctly for three months or more with zero improvement, that is your body telling you the product was never going to reach this infection, full stop.

There is also a quieter category worth naming: persistent embarrassment that is shaping your choices, skipping pedicures, avoiding sandals, choosing closed shoes in July despite the heat. That is not a medical emergency, but it is a perfectly valid reason to book an appointment rather than keep managing around it indefinitely.

