## How to Prevent Toenail Fungus From Starting or Returning

Can you actually stop this from happening again once it has cleared, or is recurrence just the cost of having had it once? The honest answer sits somewhere in between, and the habits matter more than people expect.

Keeping feet dry, treating athlete's foot promptly, and avoiding shared nail tools meaningfully reduce your risk of developing or reinfecting a <strong>toenail fungus</strong> infection. None of this is complicated, but consistency is what actually moves the needle, not any single dramatic change.

Start with moisture. Dry feet thoroughly after showering, especially between the toes, and change out of damp socks or sweaty shoes as soon as you reasonably can. Moisture-wicking socks and breathable shoe materials help more than most people give them credit for. In shared spaces, wear sandals in locker rooms, pool decks, and communal showers rather than going barefoot, since that single habit closes off one of the most common transmission routes. Disinfect your own nail clippers regularly and never share them, and if you get professional pedicures, choose a salon that visibly sterilises tools between clients.

If you currently have athlete's foot, treat it properly and promptly rather than letting it linger, since untreated skin infection on the foot is one of the most direct paths to a nail infection later. And if you have been through treatment already, recurrence is genuinely common, with some studies putting rates as high as forty percent, which is frustrating but worth knowing upfront rather than discovering by surprise. Our dedicated guide covers how to <a href='/blog/toenail-fungus/prevent-toenail-fungus-returning'>prevent toenail fungus from coming back</a> after treatment, with specific maintenance habits for the months after your nail finally clears.

